From: sunrisepeak <speakshen@163.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 06:24:15 +0900
Subject: [clang] Malformed and unresolved module directives recover in place

Collected syntax tokens exclude module directive delimiters, a directive queued after a missing BMI still owns its keywords, and diagnostics for a missing module separator stay on the directive that lacks it.

* Exclude module directive delimiters from collected C++ tokens

* fix(modules): queued directives own their keywords after missing BMI recovery

  An absent explicitly named BMI sets a sticky module-loader fatal flag. Recovery
  continues later imports, but HandleIdentifier returned their original keywords
  after queuing the same tokens in the directive suffix stream. TokenCollector
  then received duplicate source locations and spun in the measured Release build.

  Consume the original keyword whenever a C++ module directive stream was queued.
  Keep the existing fatal return when no directive stream exists. Token-collector
  and clangd regressions verify keyword counts, later symbols and edit recovery.
  The normalized historical six-missing-BMI fixture now exits normally on Linux;
  the historical Windows exception still requires native baseline qualification.

* fix(diagnostics): missing module separators stay on their directive
